  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "The passage explains the technique of Zulu riddles using two examples. In the first, the riddle describes something as 'adorned with white hip-dresses' so that listeners think of men wearing white garments when going to a wedding to dance and set others in order. The man posing the riddle repeatedly directs their thoughts toward men and questions how they could dance if their bodies touched, until he finally reveals that the subject is teeth, ordered like men and likened to white hip-dresses; the listeners admit defeat. In the second example, the tongue is described as a man in affliction because it lies among enemies, the teeth, which fight with food and often injure the tongue while grinding it. The riddle uses the word 'man' to entangle the subject so that people search among human beings rather than immediately thinking of the tongue.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "A riddle includes the description 'adorned with white hip-dresses' so that listeners think of men rather than teeth.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "White hip-dresses are worn by men when they are going to a wedding to dance and to set men in order.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "The listeners repeatedly say that the men of the riddle are men.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "The riddle-setter asks how the men could dance if their bodies touch and continually draws the listeners away from their proposed answer by words instead of merely denying it.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Eventually the riddle-setter reveals that the subject is teeth, whose order is like that of men, and that the white hip-dresses refer to the teeth.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "The listeners respond to the explanation of the riddle by saying, 'You have beaten us.'",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "The tongue is described as a man in affliction because it is in the midst of enemies.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "The teeth are described as the enemies of the tongue.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "When the teeth are eating, they fight with the food to grind it and the tongue is often injured in the process.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "In the second riddle the word 'man' is used to entangle the subject so that people search among men rather than immediately thinking of the tongue.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    }
  ],
