{"record_id":"batch.motif.african-nursery-tales-zulus-callaway-archive-l31354-l31460","source_text_path":"texts/public-domain/zulu/project-gutenberg/nursery-tales-traditions-histories-zulus-callaway.md","passage_locator":{"label":"IZINGANEKWANE. / UNTOMBI-YAPANSI. / IZINGANEKWANE. / UNTOMBI-YAPANSI.; lines 31354-31460","start":"31354","end":"31460","translation":"Nursery Tales, Traditions, and Histories of the Zulus, in Their Own Words","notes":"Stable markdown line range generated from canonical text."},"canonical_text":{"quote":"","summary":"At the home of the sister of Untombi-yapansi, a being called an imbulu arrives. The sister asks who she is, and the imbulu claims to be the younger sister, Untombi-yapansi, protesting that she is not recognised. The sister remembers only the real child's name and that her body once glistened like brass, so she is doubtful. The imbulu explains that she was very ill and has lost her former shining body, insisting she is Untombi-yapansi. The sister weeps and accepts her as the child of their house.

The sister then asks about another woman standing at the doorway. The imbulu dismisses her as a mere thing she met at the river, walking on foot. The sister offers food; she gives porridge to the imbulu, who eats. The sister then wants to give whey to the supposed servant at the doorway. The imbulu urges that it be given to her outside, but the sister’s husband objects, saying food should not be given to someone outside; the woman must be brought into the house to eat. The sister calls her in, asking her name. The imbulu names her "Umsila-wezinja" (Tail-of-dogs), and the sister invites her to eat.

