  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "In this portion of the preface, the author discusses orthographic and grammatical issues involved in writing Zulu (and Xhosa) in Roman script. He contrasts older manuscripts and cuneiform inscriptions, where words are written continuously and a special sign later allowed separation of words, with his own preference for writing individual words apart. He explains exceptions where whole sentences have \"petrified\" into single words and describes joining prepositions and possessive particles to nouns as case forms, giving examples with chiefs, personal names, and place-names. He criticizes the practice of capitalizing nominal roots (e.g., nKosi) rather than treating prefixes as essential parts of nouns, and limits capitals to paragraph starts and proper names. Finally, he describes difficulties in achieving a phonetic, uniform orthography across languages, notes objections to a universal alphabet such as Lepsius's, and states that he keeps as close as possible to existing spelling conventions.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
