    {
      "id": "ev:2",
      "type": "summary",
      "locator": "lines 497-507",
      "quote_or_summary": "The author states he has adopted a system of writing words apart, similar to other Zulu and Kccosa works, but notes exceptions where sentences have become petrified into words, such as ngani and kangaka. He also mentions writing prepositions with their governed nouns as single words, regarding them as noun cases like kuye and nami.",
      "source_text_path": "texts/public-domain/zulu/project-gutenberg/nursery-tales-traditions-histories-zulus-callaway.md",
      "rights_note": "Public domain (Project Gutenberg)."
    },
    {
      "id": "ev:3",
      "type": "summary",
      "locator": "lines 507-519",
      "quote_or_summary": "He explains that possessive particles are not separated from nouns and usually form blended possessive cases. Examples include umntwana wenkosi (“the child of the chief”) and umntwana kampande (“the child of Umpande”), which he treats as genitives of inkosi and Umpande. He also gives genitives of place, such as umuntu waselovo and umuntu wakwazulu, instead of separated forms.",
      "source_text_path": "texts/public-domain/zulu/project-gutenberg/nursery-tales-traditions-histories-zulus-callaway.md",
      "rights_note": "Public domain (Project Gutenberg)."
    },
    {
      "id": "ev:4",
      "type": "summary",
      "locator": "lines 519-539",
      "quote_or_summary": "The author discusses difficulties with capital letters, citing anomalies like capitals in the middle of words and paragraphs starting with small letters. He attributes this to not treating prefixes as essential parts of nouns and to unfamiliarity with initial changes in Zulu grammar. He criticizes using capitals to distinguish nominal roots, such as nKosi, arguing that the root Kosi has no meaning without the prefix. He concludes that prefixes were always needed to specialize root meanings and states that he has reduced capital letters to marking paragraphs and proper names in the nominative case.",
      "source_text_path": "texts/public-domain/zulu/project-gutenberg/nursery-tales-traditions-histories-zulus-callaway.md",
      "rights_note": "Public domain (Project Gutenberg)."
    },
    {
      "id": "ev:5",
      "type": "summary",
      "locator": "lines 539-560",
      "quote_or_summary": "He turns to orthography, noting that they profess to write phonetically but face objections because letters have different phonetic values across European languages and even within a single language. A uniform orthography and universal alphabet are widely desired, but he considers them unattainable at present and calls practical difficulties in using Lepsius's universal alphabet insuperable, even if its principles were sound. He says he has departed as little as possible from existing spelling.",
