{"record_id":"batch.motif.african-nursery-tales-zulus-callaway-archive-l5675-l5779","source_text_path":"texts/public-domain/zulu/project-gutenberg/nursery-tales-traditions-histories-zulus-callaway.md","passage_locator":{"label":"IZIXGAXEKWAXE. / UZEMBENI. / IZINGANEKWANE. / UZEMBENI.; lines 5675-5779","start":"5675","end":"5779","translation":"Nursery Tales, Traditions, and Histories of the Zulus, in Their Own Words","notes":"Stable markdown line range generated from canonical text."},"canonical_text":{"quote":"","summary":"In this segment of the Uzembeni tale, a powerful woman known in the English as Long-toe (linked in headings to Uzembeni) returns home and finds only one daughter. She sets out, asking where her child has gone, and pursues the fugitives until morning. At noon, Usikulumi and a damsel see the dust of Long-toe approaching. The damsel points her out and asks where they can flee. They see a lofty yellow-wood tree, run to it, and climb up into it while the dogs remain at its foot.

Long-toe/Uzembeni arrives, described as a very powerful woman, carrying an axe. She looks up, sees them in the tree, and begins to hew the tree with all her might. As she does so, the dogs bite her; when the tree is heard to creak, beginning to break, the dogs seize her and tear her body apart, ripping off her head, arm, limbs, and intestines, and dragging the pieces away.

