    "summary": "A marriage party of girls at a great bathing pool encounter the monstrous Isikgukgumadevu, which gives one girl a petticoat and keeps the others' belongings until they petition it and are allowed to depart, blaming Untombinde, the king's child, for leading them there. Untombinde refuses to beseech the monster because of her royal status, and it seizes her and puts her into the pool. The other girls return to the king and report her abduction; he recalls warning her that to the place called Ilulange nothing returns and says she now goes there forever. The king musters an army of young men to fetch the monster that killed Untombinde, but when they meet it at the river, described as as big as a mountain, it devours the army, then goes to the king's village and swallows all men, dogs, cattle, the whole country, and two beautiful, much-beloved twin children. One father escapes, arms himself with clubs and a large assagai, and sets out to kill the monster; on his way he meets buffaloes who speak, telling him he is seeking Unomabunge, O-gaur-iminga, and urging him forward. Editorial notes gloss Intontela as a military kraal, interpret the monster's honorific names as feminine and magnifying, and compare the guiding, talking beings in this quest to a similar motif in Grimm's tale \"The Godfather.\"",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
