  },
  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "“Ubongopa-kamagadhlela, Ubongopa-kamagadhlela, / Stand still now; it is the time for standing still…”",
    "summary": "A chief summons his entire tribe and orders them to follow a boy and kill him. The boy moves quickly and, when he hears they are near, repeats the phrase “Ubongopa-kamagadhlela” and declares it is time to stand still, asking if they do not see they are being killed by thieves of another tribe. The cattle stand still. The people shout for him to remain in place so they can kill him, saying he has long practised magic, and tell him to come down; he descends and is told to stand apart from the cattle so the assagais will not pierce them. They hurl assagais at him, but the weapons fail to reach him and strike the ground instead, while he jeers at them. A soldier comments that they are being worsted by a boy. The boy asks for an assagai to stab at them, they refuse, then continue throwing assagais which still fail to hit him until they admit they are worsted and invite him to try. A footnote compares this episode with the contest of Ulysses and the suitors of Penelope in Pope’s Odyssey, where Minerva turns the lances aside so they fall harmlessly.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
