    "summary": "An old woman, unable to walk, drags a water-vessel to the shade of the \"king\" and sits there, saying her limbs failed her when she tried to depart. Around noon a rock-rabbit approaches, asks who is sitting in the king's shade, hears her explanation, and tells her she will soon see Ugunggu-kubantwana. She goes to drink at a pool and returns to the shade. A duiker, then a leopard, and then many other animals arrive in turn, each asking the same question and giving the same prediction that she will soon see Ugunggu-kubantwana. As sunset approaches, very many and great animals have gathered. At sunset she hears a loud noise, becomes afraid, and trembles. Something greater than all the animals appears, and all the animals together say, \"That is Ugunggu-kubantwana.\" She sees this huge animal from a distance. Editorial notes explain that the rock-rabbit is the hyrax, the duiker is a particular antelope, and that throughout the tale the \"mother of beasts\" is called king or chief.",
