    "summary": "In a Zulu tale, a chief orders her attendants (oribes) to look among all the animals for those whose feet and legs are muddy, as a sign of which animals have eaten a woman. Mud is found on the hyenas, the oribes declare them guilty because hyenas eat people, and the hyenas are seized and taken to the chief, who eats the three hyenas. In a related episode, an old woman, who had previously fetched difficult water, waits at the border of the kraal until someone from home sees her and informs her son-in-law. The son-in-law brings her and her water-vessel into the kraal and continually drinks the water she brought. When the water is finished, the old woman declares that since she fetched the water, he must now fetch for her the liver of an ingogo, a being located far away; many loaves are baked to sustain him on this dangerous journey. Editorial footnotes compare the mud-as-witness motif to Basuto, Hottentot, and European animal tales, and compare the mutual spell-binding between the old woman and her son-in-law to the Gaelic tale of Mac Iain Direach.",
