  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "\"I am come from the men who live underground\" and the editor’s note about the priest Sönuttaro who \"dived into the earth\" to the land of Nagas.",
    "summary": "Uncama unexpectedly returns home and is recognized by his wife, who cries out in surprise and mourning, explaining that she had buried and burnt his belongings because everyone believed him dead and had sung the funeral dirge. Uncama then tells how he came from the men who live underground: he followed a porcupine into a village below, where he heard dogs baying, children crying, saw people moving about, and smoke rising. Fearing the underground people would kill him, he came back, which is why he is seen again. The narrator describes Uncama as a very small, whiskered, hairy, ugly man with many gaps in his teeth, and recalls seeing him as a boy. People continually referred to him as \"the man who went to the underground people,\" and the tale made them afraid to enter an ant-bear’s hole. An editorial note then cites a legend from Pococke’s \"India in Greece\" about the priest Sönuttaro, whose deed is said to be similar to a feat ascribed to the Zulu figure Untombi-yapansi. In that legend, a shrine is prepared for relics, and Sönuttaro, eager for a special casket of relics, dives into the earth and travels subterraneously to the land of Nagas. The Naga king seeks to retain the casket by having his son swallow it, the son becoming monstrously large and encircled by thousands of snakes. Sönuttaro miraculously creates an invisibly thin arm to remove the casket from the serpent’s stomach unnoticed, then rends the earth and returns to the upper world.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "Uncama’s wife looks at him when he returns, smites her hands, and cries.",
