      "evidence_refs": ["ev:1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "Uncama’s wife says she has buried his mat, blanket, kilt, pillow, and vessels, and burnt his blankets and mats, because she believed he was dead.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "The people start, hurry in, ask what is happening, wonder at Uncama’s arrival, and again shout the funeral dirge.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "Uncama says he has come from a distance, from the men who live underground.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Uncama reports that he followed a porcupine and came to a village.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "Uncama describes hearing dogs baying and children crying, and seeing people moving back and forth and smoke rising in the underground village.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "Uncama says he became afraid, thinking the underground people would kill him, and therefore came back, which is why he is seen that day.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "The man (Uncama) is described as very little, whiskered, hairy all over, very ugly, with many gaps in his mouth so that his teeth are no longer complete.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:3"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "The narrator says he knows Uncama, saw him when he was a boy, and that it was continually said, \"There is the man who went to the underground people.\"",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:3"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "The narrator says they were afraid to go into an ant-bear’s hole after hearing the tale that Uncama went until he reached the underground people.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:3"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "An editorial note states that in Pococke’s \"India in Greece\" there is a legend of the priest Sönuttaro who performed a feat similar to that ascribed to Untombi-yapansi.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:4"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:12",
      "text": "In the cited legend, a shrine is prepared for the reception of relics and Sönuttaro is anxious to obtain a casket of especially valuable relics to deposit in the shrine.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:4"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:13",
      "text": "Sönuttaro is said to have dived into the earth and proceeded subterraneously to the land of Nagas.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:4"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:14",
      "text": "The Naga king, wishing to keep the casket, uses his son, who swallows the casket and its contents, then extends his size to a monstrous magnitude, calls forth thousands of similar snakes, encircles himself with them, and remains coiled.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:4"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:15",
      "text": "The priest’s power and subtlety are described as overcoming the serpent’s magic; Sönuttaro miraculously creates an invisibly attenuated arm to extract the precious casket unnoticed from the Naga son’s stomach.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:4"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:16",
      "text": "After recovering the casket, Sönuttaro rends the earth and returns to the upper world.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:4"]
    }
