  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "\"The dry mingles not with the dry.\"",
    "summary": "Zarathustra asks Ahura Mazda how a man who has touched various corpses can be made clean. Ahura Mazda declares that contact with a long-dried corpse does not defile, because the dry does not mingle with the dry, and warns that if it did the world would be filled with dead beings excluded from holiness. He then sets out detailed rules for purification when a man has touched the corpse of a dog or a man: if the Nasu (corpse-pollution) has already been smitten by corpse-eating dogs or birds, a simple washing with gomez and water suffices. If Nasu has not been smitten, the worshippers of Mazda must conduct a full Barashnum rite involving three sets of three holes filled first with gomez and later with water, repeated washings, the ritual use of Ahura Mazda’s dog and fiend-smiting spells, and ensuring the man is completely dry, even to the last hair, by rubbing himself with dust. Editorial notes relate this dryness principle to Muslim Persian purity customs, and explain the relation between Sag-did (the dog’s gaze), ghosel (simple washing), and Barashnum (full purification).",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "quoted"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "Ahura Mazda answers that a man who has touched a corpse dried up and dead for more than a year \"shall\" be clean.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "Ahura Mazda states the principle \"The dry mingles not with the dry\" in connection with corpse impurity.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "Ahura Mazda explains that if the dry mingled with the dry, the material world would contain only Peshotanus, beings shut out from the way of holiness whose souls cry and wail because of the numberless deaths on earth.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "An editorial footnote reports that Muslim Persians hold that impurity from touching Christians and other idolaters is incurred only when clothes are wet, since when they are dry the impurity does not attach; consequently, Mullas and Doctors sometimes forbid Christians to go out when it rains lest accidental contact render Muslims impure.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Zarathustra asks whether a man who has touched the corpse of a dog or the corpse of a man can be made clean, addressing Ahura Mazda as \"O Maker of the material world, thou Holy One.\"",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:3"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "Ahura Mazda replies that the man can be made clean if the Nasu has already been smitten by corpse-eating dogs or corpse-eating birds, and that the man shall cleanse his body with gomez and water and be clean.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:3"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "If Nasu has not yet been smitten by corpse-eating dogs or birds, the worshippers of Mazda must dig three holes in the ground containing gomez and the unclean man must wash his body with gomez, not with water.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:4"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "During the first stage of this purification, the worshippers lift and bring Ahura Mazda’s dog in front of the man, doing so every time the unclean one passes from one hole to another, while the dog looks at the Nasu and the priest sings fiend-smiting spells.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:4"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "The worshippers of Mazda then dig three other holes, also containing gomez, and the man again washes his body with gomez, not with water, while Ahura Mazda’s dog is brought in front of him.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:5"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "After these gomez washings, they wait until the man is dried even to the last hair on the top of his head; a footnote explains that he rubs himself dry with handfuls of dust.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:5"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "They then dig three more holes in the ground containing water and the man washes his body with water, not with gomez.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:6"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:12",
      "text": "The final washing with water begins with the instruction that the man shall first wash his hands.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:7"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:13",
      "text": "Editorial notes indicate that if the Sag-did (the dog’s gaze ritual) has been performed, a simple ghosel bath is sufficient, but if Sag-did has not been performed the full Barashnum purification is necessary.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:3", "ev:7"]
    }
  ],
