    "summary": "A dangerous mother who has eaten her daughter's cheek attempts to make the others move so she can search, but they refuse, saying they know nothing and fear further harm. She points to her eaten cheek, gives up, and goes to sleep. In the morning she goes out to hunt, moving so swiftly that the dust of her departure quickly vanishes as she crosses a distant hill. While she is away, two daughters take out Usikulumi. One urges escape; the other, disfigured by her mother's injury, refuses to go, ashamed to appear before their father and resigned to being killed by a being called Long-toe. Usikulumi and the willing daughter flee together. Usikulumi fetches his dogs from a bed of reeds, and they travel day and night in fear that the mother will overtake them if they sleep, hoping to leave her behind.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
