    "summary": "The passage first explains that when a judge (Ratu) remits one-third of a person's sin or penalty, God remits the remainder, and repentance can lead to full absolution if there are no other evil deeds. It then presents a dialogue where Zarathustra asks Ahura Mazda how many men, resting closely together on adjoining carpets and pillows, become infected when one of them dies and the corpse-demon (Druj/Drug Nasu) emerges. Ahura Mazda answers that the demon rushes forth from the dead and falls on specific nearby persons, defiling a set number of them with infection, pollution, and uncleanness. The radius of impurity varies according to whether the dead is a priest, warrior, husbandman, or various types of dogs and a lynx-like animal, with higher-status humans defiling more neighbours than animals. Commentary notes that later Ravaets tradition removes these distinctions of rank and treats all corpses as equally defiling up to the eleventh neighbour, and it briefly defines some of the animals mentioned.",
